{"code":"# [debug]
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_csv('reef.csv')
print("Columns:", df.columns.tolist())
print("First few rows:")
print(df.head())
# Check summary of potential classification variables
for col in ['size', 'length', 'trophic_level', 'biomass', 'quantity']:
    if col in df.columns:
        print(f"{col} summary:")
        print(df[col].describe())
    else:
        print(f"Column '{col}' not in DataFrame.")"}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Marine protected areas (MPAs) buffer the decoupling of fish biomass production (P) from standing biomass (B) under warming: the P/B ratio has remained stable over time inside MPAs but declined on fished reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare temporal trends in the production‐to‐biomass ratio (P/B) between reefs with different protection_status.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compute P/B = prod / biomass aggregated over all transects.  \n2. Join P/B with reef metadata including protection_status (e.g. 'no‐take' vs 'open').  \n3. Visualize P/B vs year stratified by protection_status.  \n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: P_by_B ~ year * protection_status + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n5. Test if the interaction term (year:protection_status) is significant.  \n6. Check model diagnostics and plot predicted P/B trends for each protection category.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Dataframe of P/B per reef–year with protection_status.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model summary with interaction p‐value.  \n3. Plot of observed and predicted P/B over time for MPAs vs fished reefs."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Fish community temporal turnover rates differ by depth stratum: deeper transects show lower year‐to‐year beta diversity (higher stability) compared to shallow transects.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare annual species turnover among successive surveys across depth strata.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify each transect as shallow (<10 m), mid (10–20 m), or deep (>20 m) based on the depth column.  \n2. For each reef and depth stratum, assemble presence–absence matrices by year.  \n3. Compute pairwise Sørensen turnover (βsim) between consecutive years (year t vs t+1) using a beta‐diversity package.  \n4. Aggregate turnover values by depth stratum across all reefs.  \n5. Test for differences among strata using one‐way ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test.  \n6. Visualize depth‐stratum means and confidence intervals of turnover.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of mean turnover per depth stratum.  \n2. Statistical test results comparing strata.  \n3. Bar plot (or boxplot) of turnover by depth stratum."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Pelagic subsidies (proxied by mean chlorophyll) drive an increased share of planktivore production relative to benthic production across reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether reefs with higher mean_chl have a greater proportion of fish production attributable to planktivorous species.",  
      "steps":"1. Classify species as planktivores if trophic_level ≤ 3.2, others as non‐planktivores.  \n2. For each reef–year, sum prod by category and compute prop_planktivore = prod_planktivore / total_prod.  \n3. Merge prop_planktivore with mean_chl per reef–year.  \n4. Fit a mixed‐effects model: prop_planktivore ~ mean_chl + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n5. Perform significance test for mean_chl coefficient.  \n6. Plot prop_planktivore vs mean_chl with fitted regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Dataframe of prop_planktivore and mean_chl per reef–year.  \n2. Model summary showing effect of mean_chl.  \n3. Scatter plot with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Warm‐affinity species have increased in relative abundance over time, reflecting tropicalization in the Gulf of California.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Identify species’ SST affinities and test temporal trends in the abundance of warm‐affinity species.",  
      "steps":"1. For each species, calculate its mean mean_sst across all observations (species‐level SST affinity).  \n2. Classify the top 25% of species by SST affinity as warm‐affinity, bottom 25% as cool‐affinity.  \n3. For each reef–year, sum quantity of warm‐affinity species and total quantity to compute prop_warm.  \n4. Fit a linear mixed model: prop_warm ~ year + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n5. Test for a positive year effect.  \n6. Plot temporal trend in prop_warm with confidence intervals.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of species mean_sst and warm/cool classification.  \n2. Model summary for prop_warm trend.  \n3. Time series plot of prop_warm."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Mean individual fish body size per reef has decreased over time, with stronger declines on fished reefs than within MPAs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess temporal trends in mean size and how protection_status moderates those trends.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compute mean size weighted by quantity: sum(size*quantity)/sum(quantity).  \n2. Merge with protection_status.  \n3. Fit a mixed model: mean_size ~ year * protection_status + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n4. Test the interaction term.  \n5. Plot mean_size trends over time separated by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Dataframe of mean_size per reef–year with protection_status.  \n2. Mixed‐effects model summary and interaction p‐value.  \n3. Line plots of mean_size trajectories for MPAs vs fished reefs."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Functional redundancy in top trophic groups (carnivores) is declining over time: the number of carnivore species per reef is decreasing faster than for mid‐trophic groups.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare temporal trends in species richness across defined trophic‐level guilds.",  
      "steps":"1. Define guilds by trophic_level: herbivores (<2.5), omnivores (2.5–3.5), carnivores (>3.5).  \n2. For each reef–year and guild, count unique species (richness).  \n3. Fit separate mixed‐effects models for each guild: richness ~ year + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n4. Compare slopes among guilds (e.g., via contrast tests).  \n5. Visualize richness trends side‐by‐side for the three guilds.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Time series of guild‐specific species richness per reef.  \n2. Tables of slope estimates by guild with p‐values.  \n3. Multi‐panel plot of richness vs year for each guild."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher mean biomass exhibit greater stability in species evenness (lower year‐to‐year variance in Pielou’s J) than low‐biomass reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether reefs with differing biomass levels differ in temporal stability of evenness.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute mean biomass per reef across all years.  \n2. Compute Pielou’s J per reef–year as before.  \n3. For each reef, calculate the interannual variance of J.  \n4. Stratify reefs into biomass tertiles (low, mid, high).  \n5. Compare J‐variance among tertiles using ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis.  \n6. Plot reef‐level J‐variance vs mean biomass and boxplots by tertile.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Reef‐level dataset with mean biomass and J‐variance.  \n2. Statistical comparison of J‐variance across tertiles.  \n3. Scatterplot and boxplot illustrating the relationship."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Fish biomass production exhibits a unimodal (hump‐shaped) relationship with sea surface temperature (mean_sst), indicating an optimal temperature for maximum production.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for and quantify a quadratic effect of mean_sst on total fish production per reef–year.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, sum prod across all observations.  \n2. Merge with mean_sst.  \n3. Center mean_sst and create mean_sst^2 term.  \n4. Fit a mixed‐effects model: prod ~ mean_sst + mean_sst_sq + (1|region) + (1|reef).  \n5. Test significance of the quadratic term.  \n6. Estimate the SST at which predicted production is maximized.  \n7. Plot production vs SST with fitted quadratic curve.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary with coefficients for SST and SST^2.  \n2. Estimated SST optimum.  \n3. Scatterplot of prod vs mean_sst with overlaid quadratic fit curve."  
    }  
  }  
]}
